    $l22_master$> The last two chapters were about walls — a body that dismantles itself without gravity, a radiation that cannot be shielded — problems that can be slowed or managed but not solved. This one is the counterweight: the single great challenge of living off the Earth that is genuinely being *won*. It is **life support**, the machinery that makes the portable Earth real — that supplies the air a crew breathes, the water they drink, the food they eat, and, above all, *recycles* them, so that a spacecraft need not carry every gram of everything its crew will ever consume. Its central idea is one of the most beautiful in all of spaceflight: **closing the loop.**

Begin two hundred thousand miles from home, with three astronauts slowly being poisoned by their own breath. In April 1970 an oxygen tank aboard Apollo 13 exploded, and the crew abandoned the crippled command module for the lunar lander — a lifeboat built to keep *two* people alive for *two* days, now asked to keep *three* alive for *four*. The immediate killer was not a lack of oxygen; they had oxygen. It was **carbon dioxide**, the exhaled gas that builds silently in a sealed cabin until it brings headache, confusion, and death. The lander's scrubber canisters were saturating, and the spare canisters — from the command module — were the wrong shape: **square pegs for round holes**, the air the crew needed sealed inside a cartridge that would not fit the socket.

{{image: Apollo 13 | The Apollo 13 crew's jury-rigged "mailbox" — a square command-module CO₂ scrubber taped into the round lunar-module system with plastic bags, a manual cover, a sock, and duct tape. Life support in its purest form: the unforgiving management of the atoms that keep a human alive.}}

What happened next is one of the celebrated moments in engineering. A ground team was handed a duplicate of exactly what the crew had aboard — and nothing else — and told to make the square canister work in the round system. They built an adapter from plastic bags, cardboard torn from a flight-manual cover, a sock, and duct tape, radioed up the steps, and the crew taped the "mailbox" together. The carbon dioxide fell. The lesson is the whole chapter in miniature: life support is the continuous, unforgiving management of the atoms that keep a human alive, and on Earth every one of these loops is closed for us, automatically and invisibly, forever. In a spacecraft, the loops must be closed by deliberate machinery — or the air itself turns lethal, and survival comes down to a sock and duct tape.

## The metabolic ledger, and the three loops

Life support is nothing more, and nothing less, than the management of what a body takes in and gives out. So begin with the ledger. Each day, roughly, one person consumes about **0.84 kg of oxygen**, **3.5 kg of water** (the largest need by mass), and **0.6 kg of dry food**; and produces about **1.0 kg of carbon dioxide**, **3.9 kg of water** (as urine, sweat, and exhaled moisture — slightly more out than in, because metabolism itself makes water), solid waste, and a steady trickle of heat.

Hidden in that ledger is the near-symmetry that makes everything possible: **the outputs are chemically related to the inputs.** The exhaled carbon dioxide holds the carbon from the food and the oxygen from the air; the wastewater is the same water that went in. The atoms are not destroyed, only rearranged — so, in principle, they can be rearranged *back*: the carbon dioxide toward oxygen, the wastewater toward clean water. That is what closing a loop means, and it sorts the whole problem into **three loops**, in rising order of difficulty. The **air loop** — supply oxygen, remove carbon dioxide — is the most urgent (minutes to live without it) and moderately closeable by chemistry. The **water loop** — supply clean water, reclaim urine and humidity — is the largest by mass yet, remarkably, the *easiest* to close tightly. And the **food loop** — supply food, handle solid waste — is the hardest by far, because no machine can make food; only living things can.

Framing all three is a single distinction. **Open-loop** life support carries everything and discards everything: bring all the air, water, and food; vent the carbon dioxide; dump the waste. It is simple and reliable — Mercury, Apollo, and even the spent lithium-hydroxide canisters of Apollo 13 were open-loop — but its mass grows without bound with mission length, about 5 kg per person per day, some 20 tonnes for four people bound for Mars. **Closed-loop** life support instead *regenerates* the inputs from the outputs, trading that ever-growing consumable mass for a *fixed* mass of recycling machinery. For a long mission that trade wins overwhelmingly, and it is the whole thrust of advanced life support. Watch the trade directly — slide the mission length, and toggle which loops are closed:

```loopclosuremass
```

```example
loop-closure
```

## The air loop: scrubbing carbon dioxide, regenerating oxygen

The air loop has two halves — remove the carbon dioxide, supply the oxygen — and the art is in joining them into a cycle. Removing carbon dioxide the simple way is what nearly failed Apollo 13: **lithium hydroxide** binds it into a solid, $2\,\text{LiOH} + \text{CO}_2 \rightarrow \text{Li}_2\text{CO}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O}$, and the spent canister is thrown away — reliable, but open-loop, its mass climbing with every person-hour. For a long mission the scrubber must instead be *regenerable*: a molecular sieve or amine sorbent that grips carbon dioxide when cool and releases it, concentrated, when warmed — reused indefinitely, and, crucially, handing that captured carbon dioxide onward to be turned back into oxygen.

The oxygen half is supplied by splitting water with solar power, $2\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\,\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2$. This is the quiet masterstroke of the whole system: it **converts the oxygen problem into a water problem.** You no longer carry oxygen at all — you carry (and recycle) water, and electrolyse it on demand, drawing your air from a resource you already regenerate at ninety percent. Put numbers to it, and see what Sabatier then adds:

```example
oxygen-loop
```

The last open end is the exhaled carbon dioxide itself: vent it, and you throw away oxygen atoms you must replace by splitting more water. The **Sabatier reaction** closes that gap, combining the captured carbon dioxide with the hydrogen left over from electrolysis, $\text{CO}_2 + 4\,\text{H}_2 \rightarrow \text{CH}_4 + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}$ — two waste streams recombined to recover water, which is electrolysed again for oxygen. The carbon departs as vented methane. Step back and admire the cycle: the crew breathes oxygen, exhales carbon dioxide, which is captured and fed to Sabatier to recover water, which is split to regenerate oxygen and the hydrogen that feeds Sabatier again. Round and round — **oxygen → carbon dioxide → water → oxygen** — a genuine cycle powered by sunlight, recovering about half the oxygen and closing the most urgent loop halfway. It does not fully close only because the carbon escapes as methane, taking oxygen with it; advanced systems crack that methane to claw back more.

## The water loop: where recycling wins

If the air loop is a partial success, the water loop is life support's triumph, and it matters most because water is the crew's largest need by mass. Over a long voyage the numbers are brutal — a crew of four on a 1,000-day Mars mission would need, open-loop, roughly **14 tonnes** of water. It *must* be recycled, and, happily, it *can* be, because recycling water is fundamentally a **purification** problem, and purification is mature.

{{image: International Space Station | The International Space Station, where the Water Recovery System reclaims ~90% of the crew's water from urine, sweat, and cabin humidity, and electrolysis of that recycled water regenerates half the oxygen. The clearest proof that closing the loop works.}}

The crew's outputs — urine, sweat, exhaled humidity, hygiene water — are all *dirty water*, water carrying contaminants but still water. Recovering it is a matter of separation, which physics does well: distillation (evaporate the water, leave the contaminants, condense the pure vapor — spun, in microgravity, since nothing settles under gravity), filtration, and catalytic cleanup. Unlike making oxygen (energy-intensive chemistry) or making food (biology), you are not *transforming* anything, only removing what does not belong. The International Space Station's Water Recovery System does exactly this and recovers on the order of **90–93%** of its water, with upgrades pushing toward **98%**. Astronauts drink what was, days earlier, their own urine and breath — hence the cheerful, accurate line that on the station **"today's coffee is tomorrow's coffee."** It only sounds unappealing until you remember it is precisely what the Earth does: every drop you have ever drunk has cycled through the planet, through other organisms, through rain and rivers, for billions of years. The station simply runs the same cycle in days instead of eons. Work the payoff for fresh numbers:

```example
water-recycling
```

Because it is purification and not transformation, the water loop has no fundamental ceiling short of 100% — push the technology and the recovered fraction only climbs. It is the clearest proof that closing loops works, and it is doubly powerful because electrolysing that recycled water is also what regenerates the oxygen: close the water loop tightly and you help close the air loop too. The water loop is the triumph. The food loop is the wall — or rather, the frontier.

## The food loop: the cycle only life can close

Here the pattern breaks. The air and water loops close with *machines* — scrubbers, electrolysers, distillers, reactors. But **no machine can make food.** Food is not a simple chemical to be assembled on demand; it is the complex, energy-rich, structured organic matter — carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins — that only *living things* can synthesise. Chemistry can split water and recombine carbon dioxide, but it cannot build a carbohydrate from scratch or assemble a protein. That synthesis, from simple inputs and light, is something only biology does. Which is why every crewed mission to date has left the food loop essentially *open* — carrying or resupplying every calorie from Earth. The station recycles most of its water and half its oxygen; its food is flown up.

{{image: Photosynthesis | Photosynthesis: a plant builds energy-rich food from carbon dioxide, water, and light, releasing oxygen. It is the one process that closes the food loop — and, in doing so, closes the air and water loops at the same time.}}

To close the food loop, then, you must bring the one thing that makes food: living plants. And a plant is generous, because via photosynthesis, $6\,\text{CO}_2 + 6\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{light} \rightarrow \text{C}_6\text{H}_{12}\text{O}_6 + 6\,\text{O}_2$, it closes **all three loops at once** — taking up the carbon dioxide the crew exhales (air), drawing and transpiring clean water (water), releasing oxygen (air again, no electrolysis needed), and producing food (the loop no machine can touch). A garden aboard a ship is a miniature of the Earth's own biosphere, the same photosynthetic engine that has closed the planet's loops for billions of years. This is **bioregenerative life support**, and its dream is a spacecraft that is a self-sustaining world — a tiny Earth cut loose and sailing between the planets.

But it is far harder than the mechanical loops, for reasons that go to the difference between machines and living things. Plants are slow and space-hungry: feeding four people is not a window box but a substantial, power-hungry farm. Living systems are fragile and *coupled* — a machine that breaks is repaired or swapped, but an ecosystem is a web that fails in cascading, unforeseeable ways, through crop disease or pest or nutrient drift. And balancing a closed ecological loop, keeping every cycle stable at once, is one of the hardest things humans have attempted. The state of the art is a hopeful beginning: since 2015, astronauts have grown and eaten small crops — lettuce, radishes, even flowers — aboard the station, the first food ever grown and eaten in space, proving the concept and lifting morale. But growing *all* of a crew's food remains a frontier. For a sobering measure of how hard, there is one experiment every student of spaceflight should know.

## Biosphere 2: the humbling of the closed loop

In the Arizona desert stands a vast sealed enclosure of glass and steel — over three acres holding a miniature rainforest, savanna, wetland, desert, and a small ocean with a coral reef, plus a farm. It was called **Biosphere 2** (casting the Earth as Biosphere 1), and it was built to test the dream at the heart of this chapter: can humans build a sealed, self-sustaining ecological system that keeps people alive with no material input from outside? In September 1991 eight "biospherians" sealed themselves in, intending to live two years entirely on what the enclosed ecosystems could provide.

It did not work as planned, and the way it failed is the lesson. Over the first sixteen months the oxygen inside slowly, relentlessly *fell* — from 21% toward **14%**, the thin air of a 4,000-metre summit — leaving the crew weak and breathless. The cause was a cascade nobody had predicted: the rich soil chosen to grow food teemed with microbes that consumed oxygen and exhaled carbon dioxide faster than expected, and that carbon dioxide was then absorbed by the curing *concrete* of the structure rather than returned to the plants to be photosynthesised back into oxygen. The carbon cycle had a hidden leak, and the oxygen the microbes burned was simply lost. Eventually oxygen had to be pumped in from outside — the closure broken, the sealed world unable to sustain its own air. Feel how a trivial-seeming drain becomes a crisis when there is no buffer to absorb it:

```biosphere2oxygen
```

And more failed: crops came up short and the crew went hungry and thin; the pollinating insects mostly died while cockroaches and ants thrived; the ocean's chemistry drifted; and the eight hungry, oxygen-starved people split into bitter factions. Biosphere 2 is often called a failure, but that misses its worth — it was a priceless lesson in how extraordinarily hard it is to close an ecological loop, and it teaches three things. First, **living systems are bewilderingly complex and coupled**: the oxygen crash came from soil microbes, concrete chemistry, and the carbon cycle tangled together through a path no one foresaw — and you cannot reliably close a loop you do not fully understand. Second, **closure is unforgiving**: an open system buffers small imbalances in the vastness of its surroundings, but in a closed one there is no buffer, so a slow drain of half a percent a month compounds into catastrophe. Third, and deepest, it teaches **humility about the Earth** — the planet closes all these loops for eight billion people and all life, effortlessly, stably, for billions of years, and we notice its sophistication only when we build a small version and watch it fall apart. It is not a wall like radiation; it is a hard problem of engineering and biology that better understanding will eventually crack. But it marks, honestly, how far we have to go before a spacecraft can be a living world.

## The invisible loops, and the difference between visiting and living

The three headline loops are not the whole of it. A real portable Earth must also manage the flows the planet hides from us. **Heat** is the surprising one: a sealed can of warm bodies and hot electronics, wrapped in vacuum, cannot conduct or convect its heat away — it can only *radiate* it as infrared, which is why the station's big white panels are not collectors but radiators, and why thermal control in space is mostly a fight against overheating. **Humidity** must be condensed out of the air before it fogs equipment and breeds mould — and, elegantly, fed into the water loop. **Trace contaminants** — the hundreds of gases that outgas from plastics and bodies — must be continuously scrubbed, or a sealed cabin slowly turns poisonous. And **pressure and oxygen fraction** must be held in a narrow band, enough to breathe but not so oxygen-rich that everything becomes a firebomb, the lesson written in the Apollo 1 fire. Every one of these is a service the Earth performs for free, unnoticed, and every one must be rebuilt, machine by machine, to keep a crew alive even briefly.

Collect it all, and life support is, at bottom, the difference between *visiting* space and *living* there. Open the loops and you can visit — carry your supplies, stay days or weeks, come home before they run out. Close them and you can *stay* — turning a mass that grows without bound into a self-sustaining cycle. The water loop is nearly closed, the air loop half-closed, both by machinery, and those hard-won triumphs already let people live in orbit for years; the food loop, and true closure, await the marriage of engineering and biology that Biosphere 2 shows is hard but not hopeless. In a unit heavy with walls, this is the domain where the trend runs unmistakably the other way — toward easier, cheaper, possible. And its deepest gift is reverence for the original: the planet-sized biosphere that closes every one of our loops for free, so perfectly that we never notice, until we try to do it ourselves.

## Further reading

For the engineering, NASA's public descriptions of the ISS Environmental Control and Life Support System are clear and authoritative. For the biological dream, the European MELiSSA project is a good window onto bioregenerative life support. And for the humbling, Jane Poynter's *The Human Experiment* — a firsthand account by one of the eight biospherians — is a gripping read on just how hard it is to close a living loop.$l22_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
